00:06:22.555 --> 00:06:23.195
I like Good,

00:06:23.915 --> 00:06:31.035
beautiful. Well, I'd like to thank you, Mr. Chairman, providing the time on the agenda tonight to go through the results of our

00:06:31.915 --> 00:06:37.340
joint Conservation Commission and Inland Wetlands Commission spring vernal pool survey. And

00:06:38.780 --> 00:06:44.940
so what I thought I'd do is give you a you know the summary results of what we've you know what we actually found.

00:06:45.980 --> 00:06:48.140
To ground folks

00:06:47.835 --> 00:06:52.795
a year ago we worked in partnership with the University of Connecticut

00:06:53.035 --> 00:06:55.675
to map potential vernal pools

00:06:55.835 --> 00:06:57.515
town wide using

00:06:58.395 --> 00:07:00.715
aerial photographs and satellite imagery

00:07:01.350 --> 00:07:08.710
to you know identify where those potential pools might be. And if I recall, I think we identified 183

00:07:08.710 --> 00:07:10.070
potential pools,

00:07:10.390 --> 00:07:12.950
based on certain signatures on those images.

00:07:14.235 --> 00:07:16.075
We then reached out to

00:07:16.635 --> 00:07:20.875
landowners for permission for those pools that were on private land

00:07:21.515 --> 00:07:24.635
to survey because the intent was to survey pools

00:07:24.715 --> 00:07:28.235
townwide as many as we could on both private and public land.

00:07:29.080 --> 00:07:30.840
That was done and completed,

00:07:31.880 --> 00:07:36.680
and now we're and then we the final phase was to conduct

00:07:37.480 --> 00:07:40.680
spring surveys, biological and habitat assessments

00:07:40.955 --> 00:07:44.235
at the pools where potential pools where we had access,

00:07:44.795 --> 00:07:46.635
with a goal of confirming

00:07:46.795 --> 00:07:49.035
if in fact they were vernal pools

00:07:49.435 --> 00:07:51.195
based on the biology

00:07:51.195 --> 00:07:51.755
there,

00:07:52.075 --> 00:08:02.130
and also to determine if they were vernal pools to collect some biological and habitat data that could be used both by the Inland Wetlands Commission in reviewing applications,

00:08:02.450 --> 00:08:05.090
but also could be used by the Conservation Commission

00:08:05.250 --> 00:08:09.410
as it moves forward with a broader natural resource inventory

00:08:09.605 --> 00:08:10.485
mapping,

00:08:10.725 --> 00:08:12.965
you know, critical resources in the town

00:08:13.285 --> 00:08:16.245
in preparation for our 2025

00:08:16.245 --> 00:08:18.245
plan of conservation and development.

00:08:18.485 --> 00:08:20.085
So that's kind of where we are.

00:08:21.205 --> 00:08:22.085
We

00:08:22.085 --> 00:08:25.605
had, we ended up with 18 participating landowners

00:08:26.460 --> 00:08:29.340
and 15 participating volunteers.

00:08:29.820 --> 00:08:36.620
As you recall, a goal of this was to train volunteers from the town to be able to conduct the

00:08:37.180 --> 00:08:38.780
biological surveys

00:08:39.305 --> 00:08:40.265
and

00:08:40.265 --> 00:08:44.025
to, you know, to help so that we could cover a fair number of areas.

00:08:44.745 --> 00:08:48.505
Was based on work that was done in seven towns

00:08:49.065 --> 00:08:51.145
back in the early 2000s

00:08:51.690 --> 00:08:59.370
in the in the Farmington River Valley, where there was an evaluation of the ability of adult volunteers to collect vertical specific

00:09:00.010 --> 00:09:02.490
data. So we followed very similar to

00:09:03.050 --> 00:09:06.410
the methodology and the approach to that to that project.

00:09:07.665 --> 00:09:10.545
We ended up serving 124

00:09:10.545 --> 00:09:15.745
potential vernal pools, and again those at this stage they were still potential pools.

00:09:16.225 --> 00:09:24.840
Because to confirm a vernal pool, really have to do it based on the biota, and obviously you can't see that from analysis of aerial photographs and satellite images.

00:09:26.600 --> 00:09:28.120
So we had 124

00:09:28.120 --> 00:09:35.725
potential pools that we had access to out of I think a hunt, you know there was a 183 or so that we actually

00:09:36.285 --> 00:09:37.565
initially mapped.

00:09:38.605 --> 00:09:48.765
That included 51 mapped pools and 26 new pools that we identified during the survey. So, did identify quite a number of new pools that we did not find

00:09:49.360 --> 00:09:50.640
when we did the

00:09:50.960 --> 00:09:53.280
GIS analysis, the satellite imagery.

00:09:53.600 --> 00:10:03.280
And we also had six additional potential pools that in the process of going through the aerial images, we identified six that we had missed previously

00:10:03.440 --> 00:10:07.495
on the aerial images, which we were able to go out and conduct

00:10:07.495 --> 00:10:08.455
some surveys.

00:10:08.935 --> 00:10:11.575
So, long and short of it is we confirmed

00:10:11.575 --> 00:10:12.855
77

00:10:13.015 --> 00:10:15.175
pools based on the biology

00:10:16.055 --> 00:10:16.935
and

00:10:17.175 --> 00:10:19.335
47 that were not vernal pools.

00:10:19.930 --> 00:10:28.490
The primary reason for a pool not being a pool was mostly, I would say the majority of them, probably greater than 90%,

00:10:28.810 --> 00:10:31.450
where they were too shallow. They were depressions

00:10:31.450 --> 00:10:35.735
usually associated with other wetlands or along a water course

00:10:35.895 --> 00:10:38.855
that did not hold water long enough to support

00:10:39.095 --> 00:10:44.055
the biological activity necessary for fertile pool obligate species.

00:10:44.455 --> 00:10:46.535
We did have a couple of false

00:10:46.775 --> 00:10:48.215
positives with

00:10:48.455 --> 00:10:53.710
conifer trees that we thought were pools, turned out to be a hemlock tree, and a couple that were

00:10:54.830 --> 00:11:01.310
shadows. One was a ledge shadow that showed up that looked like a pool on an image, and another was a tree shadow.

00:11:01.550 --> 00:11:07.575
And then we had a few that were turned out to be ponds. The opposite of not holding water long enough,

00:11:07.975 --> 00:11:10.215
they hold water permanently,

00:11:10.535 --> 00:11:17.175
so they don't support the typical biological community associated with fertile pools, which have a temporary

00:11:18.080 --> 00:11:19.360
hydrology to them.

00:11:20.400 --> 00:11:23.680
Then of course there were, and this was sort of anticipated,

00:11:23.680 --> 00:11:24.480
especially

00:11:24.560 --> 00:11:28.640
we were somewhat conservative when we were identifying potential pools,

00:11:29.440 --> 00:11:36.125
wanting to, you know, not miss some. We knew that there were areas that would be too shallow, and we knew that there were areas that would probably be ponds,

00:11:36.205 --> 00:11:37.805
but we wanted to hit them anyway.

00:11:39.405 --> 00:11:47.670
There were 92 pools that we initially mapped that we did not have access to, so we're not able to survey.

00:11:47.910 --> 00:11:57.590
There were a couple that we did have access to, but actually just couldn't get to because of some flood conditions or some extremely thick vegetation or a combination thereof.

00:11:57.990 --> 00:12:05.635
So by and large, a 124 potential pools were surveyed, 77 were confirmed this fertile pools and 47 not.

00:12:06.595 --> 00:12:07.875
The

00:12:07.875 --> 00:12:17.570
surveys really were broken at the primary focus of the spring survey was to look at the biological value of the pool, And this is based on the work by

00:12:18.690 --> 00:12:21.090
Calhoun and Clemens in 2005,

00:12:21.490 --> 00:12:32.605
where they put together a method that's fairly widely used in the Northeast and here in Connecticut. It's called the best development practices for conserving vernal pools. It was targeted at collecting

00:12:32.685 --> 00:12:38.205
some basic habitat data and biological data to inform municipal commissions

00:12:38.445 --> 00:12:39.165
to

00:12:39.325 --> 00:12:55.010
more effectively conserve pools. So that's the methodology that we were following. There were some slight deviations that we made additions, and I'll explain those as I go through and why. But pretty much we're following the core principles set forth in their biological surveys and habitat surveys.

00:12:55.725 --> 00:13:01.805
And the focus of the biological surveys in their in that plan is to look at the for the presence of any

00:13:02.205 --> 00:13:05.405
state listed species that might occur in the pools,

00:13:06.525 --> 00:13:10.045
or what are called vertical indicator or obligate species.

00:13:10.520 --> 00:13:14.280
And those are a series of amphibians and one

00:13:15.080 --> 00:13:21.320
shrimp freshwater shrimp called the fairy shrimp that require vertical pools, so they're determined to be obligate species.

00:13:21.640 --> 00:13:27.125
So we're looking for the presence of them, and then we're trying to get a sense of their abundance,

00:13:27.445 --> 00:13:37.445
and that's where the egg mass counts come in. In this case, particular other, at least 25 egg masses, although we tended to try to count as many egg masses as we could to get a better idea

00:13:38.750 --> 00:13:42.270
So, the spring surveys focused on those biological surveys.

00:13:43.470 --> 00:13:44.830
The first survey,

00:13:45.550 --> 00:13:49.390
we have to time those surveys based on the breeding activity for

00:13:50.030 --> 00:13:52.590
the amphibians as they move forward in the spring.

00:13:53.175 --> 00:13:54.535
Luckily,

00:13:54.695 --> 00:13:56.135
this spring followed

00:13:56.135 --> 00:13:58.535
an almost ideal trajectory

00:13:58.615 --> 00:14:06.135
where the breeding season started on target and did not shut down with inclement weather and continued in a very

00:14:06.550 --> 00:14:20.310
typical fashion, which actually is not so typical or in in New England. We were lucky in the fact that we could time the survey very effectively and we we hit it just as they were. The species were starting to breed.

00:14:20.945 --> 00:14:23.745
So the first survey that we worked with volunteers,

00:14:24.225 --> 00:14:34.145
their focus was to was a March survey. They had to complete the first survey of the vernal pool in March with a focus on identifying where their wood frog egg masses in the pools.

00:14:35.110 --> 00:14:37.910
And trying to get a count of those egg masses.

00:14:38.710 --> 00:14:40.150
Second survey

00:14:40.950 --> 00:14:47.590
whispered targeted for April because spotted salamanders tend to breed a little bit later and have a more protracted breeding season.

00:14:48.230 --> 00:14:50.550
So the second survey focused on

00:14:50.845 --> 00:14:55.965
spotted salamander egg masses. Are they present and trying to get an abundance count of those?

00:14:57.085 --> 00:14:58.045
In addition,

00:14:58.765 --> 00:15:04.445
the volunteers did not do this, but I did this. I visited all the pools and I did a dip net survey

00:15:04.790 --> 00:15:07.030
both during March and April

00:15:07.510 --> 00:15:15.830
to look for the presence of marbled salamander larvae. And the reason we did not have the volunteers do that is that's more difficult technique to do.

00:15:16.550 --> 00:15:17.910
It's harder for identification,

00:15:18.635 --> 00:15:19.675
And also,

00:15:19.995 --> 00:15:25.195
there's a potential to spreading disease if you're running a lot of dip nets through a multitude of

00:15:26.795 --> 00:15:27.995
vertical pools.

00:15:28.235 --> 00:15:33.515
So I was able to do that by using multiple dip nets and doing a disinfecting protocol

00:15:33.675 --> 00:15:36.290
between surveys so it controlled

00:15:36.290 --> 00:15:38.210
the number of people who were doing that.

00:15:38.690 --> 00:15:41.250
So I did that in both March and April surveys.

00:15:42.450 --> 00:15:47.490
All surveys, both the volunteers and myself, we would look for the presence of state listed species.

00:15:47.815 --> 00:15:55.495
You know, did we encounter certain turtles or snakes or certain species that we would note their occurrence because that obviously is important to know.

00:15:56.375 --> 00:16:06.190
And then I added based on some recent literature and recent studies that have been coming at, I was curious on how widely distributed another species of salamander

00:16:06.190 --> 00:16:08.110
the four toed salamander was,

00:16:08.430 --> 00:16:09.630
although it's not.

00:16:10.830 --> 00:16:12.830
An obligate vertical species,

00:16:12.910 --> 00:16:16.590
it does breed in certain vertical pools, where there's sphagnum swamp growing

00:16:17.085 --> 00:16:24.045
and we're look based on the research that we're seeing, that's a species that we may need to focus on in the future

00:16:24.845 --> 00:16:27.165
because of changing environmental conditions

00:16:27.645 --> 00:16:36.280
associated with our wetlands. So I thought it was a good opportunity to collect information on the presence of that species that are in some of these systems as well.

00:16:38.680 --> 00:16:41.800
So here is a here's sort of what we found.

00:16:43.000 --> 00:16:46.815
This this graph shows you by the different species,

00:16:47.135 --> 00:16:50.255
the percentage of vertical pools that they occurred in.

00:16:50.655 --> 00:16:55.135
With the wood frog they were widely distributed they occurred in 96%

00:16:55.135 --> 00:16:58.335
of the vertical pools that we confirmed

00:16:58.780 --> 00:17:02.700
Spotted salamanders a little less widely distributed in 66%

00:17:02.700 --> 00:17:03.900
of the vertical pools.

00:17:04.140 --> 00:17:05.980
That's not necessarily surprising.

00:17:06.460 --> 00:17:10.540
Wood frogs have a very short breeding season, so they're able to use

00:17:11.125 --> 00:17:15.845
pools that dry up earlier, so they can be you tend to find them in a broader

00:17:16.245 --> 00:17:17.525
range of

00:17:18.485 --> 00:17:21.525
vertical pool hydrologists than you do spotted salamanders,

00:17:21.765 --> 00:17:24.005
which have require the

00:17:24.005 --> 00:17:25.845
pool to hold water for a longer period of time.

00:17:26.490 --> 00:17:30.570
Marbled salamanders and fairy shrimp were very limited in distribution.

00:17:31.130 --> 00:17:35.370
The marbled salamander only in 5% and the fairy shrimp in 4%.

00:17:35.610 --> 00:17:38.410
I believe, certainly with the marbled salamander,

00:17:38.410 --> 00:17:39.930
that may be an under representation.

00:17:40.665 --> 00:17:41.865
The previous

00:17:42.185 --> 00:17:47.625
year we had a very wet fall and the breeding conditions were odd for marbled salamanders.

00:17:48.025 --> 00:17:53.305
