00:40:07.655 --> 00:40:10.535
up and downstream within those corridor systems.

00:40:10.855 --> 00:40:23.470
The other thing I want to note is while many of our pools were forested pools, there were some that were man made because believe it or not, made pool, you know, old farm ponds and places like that. In fact, there was one area in over

00:40:24.030 --> 00:40:28.750
by the Bishop Swamp area on the backside where it was some old forest roads where

00:40:29.115 --> 00:40:46.570
the the road was cut in so deep where there was a where there were very abundant egg masses in the in the in the tire ruts. Were holding water for an enormously long period of time and actually functioning as had higher numbers of egg masses than the natural pool that was within 100 feet of it.

00:40:46.890 --> 00:40:49.130
So it goes to show you that you know these

00:40:49.370 --> 00:40:54.970
animals can adapt to some of those type of systems. Some man made pools can function very highly.

00:40:57.175 --> 00:41:00.695
What I want you to notice is the ones that I circled the 56%

00:41:00.855 --> 00:41:03.735
the open emergent and open shrub pools.

00:41:04.055 --> 00:41:08.215
When I mean open what I mean is open canopy they're non forested

00:41:08.455 --> 00:41:15.060
so in it those types of pools, because they have a lot more sunlight, they have a lot more vegetation

00:41:15.060 --> 00:41:16.100
productivity,

00:41:16.500 --> 00:41:17.300
algae,

00:41:17.860 --> 00:41:19.380
grasses, and sedges,

00:41:20.100 --> 00:41:26.065
and you'll often get very different array of species. Some will be similar to the forested pools,

00:41:26.305 --> 00:41:30.785
but you'll get species that will utilize them that that will not utilize the forested pools.

00:41:30.945 --> 00:41:33.425
So from a broader spectrum of biodiversity,

00:41:33.425 --> 00:41:34.785
they can be very important.

00:41:35.265 --> 00:41:35.905
The

00:41:36.065 --> 00:41:38.145
fact that they're underrepresented

00:41:38.465 --> 00:41:43.270
makes the mix also makes them very valuable, so they they require

00:41:43.270 --> 00:41:45.430
some important consideration

00:41:45.670 --> 00:41:51.030
when we when we look at projects and we look at our conservation planning. If we have an open canopy pool

00:41:51.705 --> 00:41:59.065
associated with a floodplain or a forested area that may be functioning from a biodiversity standpoint at a very important level.

00:41:59.465 --> 00:42:00.825
So connected pools,

00:42:00.985 --> 00:42:03.065
high connection to wetlands and watercourses,

00:42:03.305 --> 00:42:07.680
and low occurrence of open canopy pools, which are important

00:42:07.920 --> 00:42:08.640
in

00:42:09.120 --> 00:42:10.560
conservation planning.

00:42:11.920 --> 00:42:15.120
The other thing I wanted to point to, which is also becoming

00:42:16.880 --> 00:42:18.960
evident among conservation biologists

00:42:19.215 --> 00:42:24.735
is if this is a pie chart of the estimated length of time that the pools hold water.

00:42:25.295 --> 00:42:34.840
And if you notice, and I based it on a number of criteria, one of them was you know to deter and I see I forgot a spell check here. Got the French version of summer.

00:42:36.040 --> 00:42:40.200
Do they dry up in early summer, mid summer or late summer? Okay,

00:42:41.000 --> 00:42:43.720
if they're drying up in early summer, which basically means June,

00:42:44.205 --> 00:42:50.525
they're not going to support something like spotted salamanders because they just can't develop in that length of time.

00:42:51.565 --> 00:42:53.245
If they're drying by midsummer,

00:42:53.245 --> 00:42:58.600
it's a crapshoot. Midsummer generally means sometime in mid July, you know, usually

00:42:58.920 --> 00:43:01.640
the wood frogs can get out of that for sure,

00:43:02.040 --> 00:43:04.120
but spotted salamanders it's crapshoot.

00:43:05.080 --> 00:43:09.560
Especially if it's completely forested pool, a lot of canopy and shade. If it's late summer,

00:43:09.720 --> 00:43:12.760
a lot of things can get out of it. It's cold in water for a long period of time.

00:43:13.595 --> 00:43:16.075
And by the way, this can vary year by year,

00:43:16.315 --> 00:43:20.795
depending on the you know the type of year that we have and the amount of precipitation

00:43:20.795 --> 00:43:23.355
and so forth. But in general,

00:43:23.595 --> 00:43:26.555
the trajectory of these will follow one of these sort of courses.

00:43:29.070 --> 00:43:34.270
Ideally, you have a range of those hydro periods is going to give you your greatest biodiversity,

00:43:34.590 --> 00:43:36.670
because if you have short period pools,

00:43:37.310 --> 00:43:47.465
they you know the wood frogs and the the fairy shrimp and species that are using them don't have the competition as some of the other species, so they may be very effective for them. So you want a diversity

00:43:47.945 --> 00:43:50.825
of hydro periods of your rural pools is ideal,

00:43:51.065 --> 00:43:54.745
and we tend to see that on the landscape as represented by this pie chart.

00:43:55.225 --> 00:43:55.785
However,

00:43:56.025 --> 00:43:58.185
under changing environmental conditions

00:43:58.650 --> 00:43:59.850
and modeling,

00:44:00.170 --> 00:44:03.130
those pools that hold water for a long period of time,

00:44:03.450 --> 00:44:05.850
late summer, which are only 13%

00:44:05.850 --> 00:44:07.530
of the pools that we identified,

00:44:09.210 --> 00:44:10.890
become extremely important

00:44:11.205 --> 00:44:18.005
because it may be that under changing environmental conditions, what the modeling is showing that those early summer and mid summer pools

00:44:18.085 --> 00:44:21.525
may not be very effective in supporting much biodiversity

00:44:21.525 --> 00:44:27.700
in the future. It may shift on that spectrum and the late summer pools may become very important what we

00:44:27.700 --> 00:44:29.220
call a refugia

00:44:29.540 --> 00:44:31.300
in many years for

00:44:31.380 --> 00:44:33.060
vertical breeding species.

00:44:33.540 --> 00:44:35.380
So they may play a

00:44:35.940 --> 00:44:37.780
very important role

00:44:37.940 --> 00:44:38.900
on the landscape,

00:44:39.515 --> 00:44:44.395
especially where they're connected to other pools and other networks of pools.

00:44:44.635 --> 00:44:48.315
They may be especially important because they will serve as refugium,

00:44:48.635 --> 00:44:49.995
which gets back to

00:44:50.395 --> 00:44:53.115
the statistic I showed you of the 64%

00:44:53.115 --> 00:44:56.420
of the pools connected to wetlands and watercourses.

00:44:56.420 --> 00:45:01.540
One of the interesting things I found out about this, and you find this by mucking about in the pools,

00:45:01.860 --> 00:45:05.780
is those pools that were connected to wetlands and watercourses were very mucky.

00:45:06.415 --> 00:45:08.655
And if you have a very mucky pool,

00:45:08.895 --> 00:45:26.390
even though it starts to dry up, it holds water longer. The muck stays wet. You get pockets of water in there, and I'd saw this writ large to me again with me and another biologist. We're doing a project over in Middletown from town, and we surveyed a series of 15 pools in this forested area,

00:45:26.870 --> 00:45:29.270
and we surveyed it in mid July

00:45:29.590 --> 00:45:36.470
and a fair number of pools would dry it up. There were several pools that had the longer hydro period, were clearly refugia,

00:45:36.845 --> 00:45:42.445
but there were a number of them that were mucky pools that still had small pockets

00:45:42.605 --> 00:45:46.685
of shallow water, and I'm just talking like an inch of water, and they were scattered,

00:45:46.765 --> 00:45:54.150
yet they still had larvae in them. The larvae can persist in them. Now depending on whether we get rains, you know, whether they would persist,

00:45:54.310 --> 00:45:57.830
but that mucky characteristic of those wetland associated

00:45:57.990 --> 00:46:07.135
watercourse associated pools may enable them to have a hydrology or persist a bit longer than they normally would if they were isolated

00:46:07.135 --> 00:46:11.855
and were draining a little bit more readily based on the soils not being as mucky.

00:46:12.175 --> 00:46:12.735
So

00:46:13.535 --> 00:46:17.615
those two factors are important to consider as we look at long term planning.

